            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nAda Lovelace was born on December 10, 1815, as Augusta Ada Byron, the only legitimate child of the poet Lord Byron and his wife Anne Isabella Milbanke. She had an affluent upbringing and received an education focused on mathematics and logic, which was unusual for a woman of her time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction to Babbage and His Work</strong><br>\nIn 1833, Lovelace was introduced to Charles Babbage and became fascinated with his work on the mechanical general-purpose computer, the Analytical Engine. She understood the potential of the machine beyond numerical calculations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration with Babbage</strong><br>\nLovelace started working with Babbage in 1842 when she translated an article by Italian mathematician Luigi Federico Menabrea on the Analytical Engine. She added her own notes, which were three times longer than the original article and presented many forward-thinking concepts.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The First Computer Program</strong><br>\nWithin her notes, Lovelace described an algorithm for the Analytical Engine to compute Bernoulli numbers. This is considered the first written computer program, although the machine to run it was never built in her lifetime.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vision for Computing</strong><br>\nLovelace foresaw the multi-purpose functionality of the modern computer. She predicted that machines like the Analytical Engine could one day manipulate symbols in accordance with rules and even create music or art, showcasing her prescient understanding of computer programming.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy in Computing</strong><br>\nAda Lovelace died at the age of 36 on November 27, 1852, but her legacy lives on. She is often cited as the first computer programmer, and her work on the Analytical Engine laid the groundwork for the computers we use today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
