          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nFor many years, Lovelace's contributions to computing were not widely recognized. However, in the latter part of the 20th century, her notes were republished, and she began to be acknowledged for her role in the history of computers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ada Lovelace Day</strong><br>\nAda Lovelace Day is celebrated on the second Tuesday of October to recognize the achievements of women in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM). This day aims to increase the visibility of female role models in STEM fields.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cultural Impact</strong><br>\nThe U.S. Department of Defense named a computer language \"Ada\" in her honor in 1979. Lovelace's work and her approach to science and mathematics continue to inspire many in the fields of computing and technology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
